<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Per-row "ticket" pill for items inside the Polaris widget
  ListView. Amber-400 fill (#FBBF24, same hue as the brand pill)
  framed by a slate-900 outline that is intentionally THICKER on
  the bottom than on the other three sides — a neobrutalist offset
  that reads as a soft drop-shadow without needing a real shadow
  layer (RemoteViews ignores `elevation`).

  Why a layer-list instead of a centered `<stroke>`?
   * `<stroke>` is centered on the path — half of its width sits
     OUTSIDE the view bounds. Inside a ListView (which has
     `clipChildren=true` by default and ignores attempts to turn
     it off), that outer half gets clipped, so the top edge of
     the first row's stroke disappears. Layered insets keep the
     entire frame inside the row's bounds, fixing the clipping.
   * Per-side insets let us make the bottom border thicker than
     the top/left/right, which `<stroke>` alone cannot do.

  Geometry:
   * Outer black layer: 16dp corner radius, fills the whole row.
   * Inner amber layer: inset 3dp on top/left/right and 6dp on
     bottom. The inner radius is reduced by the inset so the
     curve stays roughly concentric with the outer one.
   * Bottom-corner radius on the amber layer is reduced by the
     larger bottom inset (16 - 6 = 10dp) so the inner shape's
     bottom corners do not visually flatten against the outer.

  Why these insets and not 2dp / 5dp (the previous values)?
   The launcher's RemoteViews rasterizer applies anti-aliasing
   to nested rounded drawables, which can swallow up to ~1dp at
   each edge of a thin inset band. With a 2dp top inset that AA
   smear made the top frame visually disappear while the 5dp
   bottom band stayed crisp. Bumping the floor to 3dp gives the
   AA enough room to bleed without erasing the band; the 6dp
   bottom keeps the visual hierarchy (bottom ~2× the rest) that
   reads as a neobrutalist drop-shadow.

  Pairs with `polaris_widget_item_icon_box.xml` and the dark-on-
  amber text colors set in `polaris_widget_item.xml`.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <item>
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="#0F172A" />
            <corners android:radius="16dp" />
        </shape>
    </item>

    <item
        android:left="3dp"
        android:top="3dp"
        android:right="3dp"
        android:bottom="6dp">
        <shape android:shape="rectangle">
            <solid android:color="#FBBF24" />
            <corners
                android:topLeftRadius="13dp"
                android:topRightRadius="13dp"
                android:bottomLeftRadius="10dp"
                android:bottomRightRadius="10dp" />
        </shape>
    </item>
</layer-list>
